WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.102
In a recent opinion piece on CNN.

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:03.803
Com Van,

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:06.006
you wrote, The police who killed Tyre

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:07.107
Nichols were black,

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:09.209
but they might still have been driven

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:10.777
by racism.

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:12.645
And I was struck by the part of the piece

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.114
where you wrote this.

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:15.749
One of the sad facts

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.650
about anti-black racism

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:18.752
is that black people

00:00:18.752 --> 00:00:20.186
ourselves are not immune

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:21.654
to its pernicious effects.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:22.789
Society's message

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:24.791
that black people are inferior,

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:28.261
unworthy and dangerous is pervasive over

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.029
many decades.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.630
Numerous experiments have shown

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.598
that these ideas

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:33.633
can infiltrate black minds

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:34.868
as well as white.

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:37.837
Self hatred is a real thing.

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:40.907
So you believe

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:41.741
this racial

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:42.709
bias played a role in

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:44.944
the officer's behaviors?

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:45.845
Yes, I do.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:49.449
Because first of all, black

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.851
people are at risk from police

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:54.654
no matter what color black, white, brown.

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:55.755
You talk to African-Americans,

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.058
they'll tell you it doesn't matter.

00:00:58.058 --> 00:00:59.726
There's this pervasive view

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.094
from law enforcement

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:02.762
that if you're black or dangerous

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.330
and I think some people think, oh,

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.999
also a white police officer

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:07.167
going after a black person,

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.002
you understand the racial implications.

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:10.370
The problem is

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.738
there are whole neighborhoods

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:12.806
that in many cities

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:13.506
are just considered

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.608
by police departments of war zones

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:17.377
and anything goes there.

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:18.511
And so

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:19.412
I think we have to have

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.947
a more nuanced view

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:24.117
of what's going on to root out a racism.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.119
It's not just about having black cops

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:27.420
or white cops or brown cops.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.822
It's about having cops

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:30.824
that are trained to respect everyone

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:31.758
and who are disciplined

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:33.093
and punished when they don't.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.462
And we don't have that in place.

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:36.396
Black people are at risk

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:37.764
no matter the color of the police.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:38.198
Officer.

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:39.632
If for some reason

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.034
your opinion

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:42.235
has been a bit controversial,

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:42.669
at least from what

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:44.070
I've seen on social media.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.207
What has the backlash sounded like and

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:48.141
what is your race,

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.410
your response to your critics?

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:50.877
Oh, well, listen,

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.846
I think some people say, hey, listen,

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:54.581
you guys just want to find race

00:01:54.581 --> 00:01:55.381
no matter what.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.683
You're just going to scream racism,

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:57.884
even if it's all black again,

00:01:57.884 --> 00:01:58.885
scream racism.

00:01:58.885 --> 00:01:59.686
And I understand

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:00.720
it does seem weird

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.621
when we're saying

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.022
there is a racial dimension here,

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:03.990
even though the police

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.225
are African-American.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:06.259
But I think what's important

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.527
for us to be able to do is

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:09.462
this is about power.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:10.597
It's not about black.

00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:11.898
It's about blue.

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:12.966
It's about power.

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:13.533
And when you have

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.635
when you have police officers who know

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:16.803
in certain neighborhoods,

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.505
they better not act that way.

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:19.472
But in other neighborhoods,

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.007
they can't act that way.

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:22.475
That's about power.

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:24.010
That's about domination.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.478
And so I think it's important

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:25.879
that we use

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:26.980
this as an opportunity

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.883
to get more nuanced and more clear

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:31.251
about the dangers

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:32.385
that African-Americans face

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:33.887
from police officers, period,

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:35.755
when there is not accountability.

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:35.889
Yeah.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:36.656
And you also make

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:37.490
the point, Van,

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:38.791
that at the end of the day,

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.827
it is the race of the victim

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:41.861
who is brutalized.

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:43.463
I'm quoting your article,

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:45.465
not the race of the violent cop

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.534
that is most relevant in determining

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:49.302
determining whether racial bias

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.804
is a factor in police violence.

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:55.108
How do we as a society

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:56.543
all the way from the federal government

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:58.178
down to individual police departments

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.145
in our communities

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:01.948
prevent this from happening again

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:03.216
from adding to this long

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:04.951
list of black deaths?

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:06.452
Unfortunately, tragically,

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:07.854
in this country?

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:10.390
You know, unfortunately, after

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.257
George Floyd,

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:13.293
we had people marching of all colors.

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:15.662
There were Black Lives Matter protests

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:16.796
where there were no black people.

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:17.897
There were people of all colors,

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:18.932
white people saying

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:19.933
something needs to be done.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.935
Unfortunately, the federal government

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:23.036
still has not passed

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:24.737
a single piece of legislation

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.073
since George Floyd to make a difference.

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:28.374
Thankfully,

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.410
President Biden did do x

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.411
some executive orders.

00:03:31.411 --> 00:03:32.412
It's not enough.

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:33.513
And so I think it's time now

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.148
for us to relook.

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:37.083
Here we are almost three years later,

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:39.686
there has been no federal change

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:43.056
in the law to make this stuff go away.

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:44.924
And so I think that

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.159
this should be a chance

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:47.961
for us to relook at it.

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:49.329
I also just want to say,

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:49.862
you know,

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:50.863
human rights abuses

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:52.131
happen all around the world.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:54.367
And often the human rights abusers

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.069
look exactly like the people

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:57.804
they're abusing the Philippines,

00:03:57.804 --> 00:03:59.973
what the law enforcement's doing there

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:00.740
with this

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.075
unbelievable attack

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:04.244
on the community around drug enforcement.

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:05.812
Those people, all look the same.

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:06.913
They talk the same.

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:07.547
Human rights

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:10.216
abuses are about power, often

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.552
discredited and marginalized.

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:14.621
Minorities get the brunt of it.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:16.322
But it's about power.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.490
And so we have to start saying

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:18.992
we don't want human rights abuses

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:21.160
in the United States from any color cop.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.629
And we don't want the black.

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:24.897
We continue to bear the brunt of it.

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:26.933
You know, I know

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.135
that a pivotal moment in your life

00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:30.870
as a human being, as a black man

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.906
and also as a lawyer was the Rodney

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:36.909
King beating, what, 30 some years ago.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:37.877
I remember

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.279
being in California

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.414
watching these images as a kid play out.

00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:43.383
Yes, very traumatizing.

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:44.384
And I know you dedicated

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:46.286
much of your career

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:49.422
to to helping reform the system.

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:50.757
I'm just curious

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:54.560
to know what impact this Tyree

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.399
Nichols case has had on you as a human.

00:04:59.532 --> 00:04:59.732
You know,

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:00.900
because I've seen reactions

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.935
from the black community,

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:03.970
from friends of mine

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:04.537
who told me

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.272
that they refused to watch the video.

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:06.673
They couldn't

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:07.807
do it emotionally

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:09.475
because it would just bring them back

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:10.677
to reliving

00:05:10.677 --> 00:05:11.477
the trauma

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:12.078
of seeing yet

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:14.247
another black person

00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:16.082
being treated in this way.

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:17.250
Did you watch the video

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.452
and how how has this

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:20.920
changed you or impacted you?

00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:22.422
Well, you know,

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:23.756
I wouldn't have watched it

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:25.958
except I had to because I work at CNN.

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:27.660
So we all watched it together

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.662
and we all have the same impact.

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:31.030
You know, this is it's hard

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.233
because, you know, I have black sons

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:38.371
and they're not unlike Tyree.

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:41.941
You know, Tyree is a skateboard kid.

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:43.343
He's

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:46.612
a Starbucks aficionado.

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:49.082
He's an Instagram photographer.

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:51.250
He's he's he's a good kid.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.518
He was a good kid.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:54.520
And I got a kid that loves know,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.223
rip stick and and Starbucks and all.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:06:00.827
And it's every black parent's nightmare

00:06:01.461 --> 00:06:02.261
that you're a kid

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:04.297
who has never been in trouble before,

00:06:04.297 --> 00:06:06.099
gets somehow in a situation

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:08.101
where his humanity cannot be seen

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.536
and his humanity cannot be honored.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.038
And you saw him.

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:12.739
You were saying,

00:06:12.739 --> 00:06:15.541
you know, sir, and hey, calm down.

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.578
The only calm voice in that entire thing

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:21.647
was Tyree trying to calm the police down

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:22.582
and the fighting,

00:06:22.582 --> 00:06:24.250
the brutality got so bad,

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.352
the pepper spray, which is a horrifically

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:28.621
caustic chemical, got to him.

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:29.822
He jumped up and ran,

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:31.824
and it cost him his life.

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:32.191
You know,

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:33.292
that should not happen

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:35.161
for a traffic stop.

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:39.065
And so, again, it rips back the scars.

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:41.334
It rips off the scabs.

00:06:41.334 --> 00:06:42.769
My sister said she couldn't watch it.

00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:44.737
A lot of people just couldn't watch it.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:46.139
I frankly wouldn't have watched it,

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:47.340
except it was my job.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.908
But to see him

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:50.977
being pummeled and beaten and stood up

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:52.211
and punched in the face over

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:54.414
and over again, it was horrifying.

00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:56.883
And, you know, I

00:06:57.383 --> 00:06:59.385
I hope this galvanized some action.

00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:00.620
I am proud

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.255
that the city of Memphis,

00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:03.723
after George Floyd

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:04.991
changed some of their rules

00:07:04.991 --> 00:07:06.692
and regulations and policies

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:08.461
so they could take swift action.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:09.962
And you saw that swift action,

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:10.997
but the swift action shouldn't

00:07:10.997 --> 00:07:11.798
just come in

00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:13.800
this one case with by black cops.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.401
People are being beaten right

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:18.070
now, conceivably in similar situations.

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:19.705
They may have a video camera,

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:21.107
but bad things are happening

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:21.941
and we need a chance

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:22.942
to do something about it.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:23.676
Yeah, something

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:25.378
absolutely needs to change.

00:07:25.378 --> 00:07:25.945
Van Jones,

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.980
really appreciate this conversation.

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:28.748
Thank you so much.

